<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- EyeGuard Find My cross-check (2026-08-31) -- see
     eyeguard/findmy_watcher.py's module docstring for the full reasoning.
     Installed to /Library/LaunchAgents (root-owned, so the monitored user
     runs it but can't edit it -- same pattern as com.eyeguard.monitor.plist),
     runs as the monitored user (no root needed -- only reads a credential
     file in the user's own data directory). LimitLoadToSessionType Aqua,
     matching com.eyeguard.monitor.plist's proven-working setting.

     LABEL RENAMED A SECOND TIME, com.eyeguard.findmywatcher ->
     com.eyeguard.findmycheck (2026-09-02). The first rename (from
     com.eyeguard.findmy, 2026-09-01) fixed a poisoned-registration
     "Bootstrap failed: 5: Input/output error" -- but findmywatcher then
     accumulated its OWN crash-loop history (a real bug: pyicloud's internal
     requests calls had no timeout and could hang indefinitely, confirmed
     live; see eyeguard/findmy_watcher.py's socket.setdefaulttimeout(30)
     fix) before that bug was found and fixed. Once findmywatcher had
     crash-looped enough times, IT got poisoned the identical way findmy
     originally did -- bootstrap failing with the same I/O error even after
     a full reboot, disable/enable cycling, and a fresh copy of an
     already-fixed plist/binary. Confirmed this is really about crash-loop
     HISTORY poisoning a label's registration, not anything about the label
     string itself or the file on disk.

     Renaming again, now that the underlying hang bug is actually fixed
     (this label should never crash-loop the way findmywatcher did), is the
     fix that already worked once for exactly this problem. If this EVER
     happens a third time, the underlying code should be suspect first --
     a label that crash-loops enough times seems to reliably poison itself
     on this Mac's launchd/BTM state, so a fresh label only buys a clean
     slate, it doesn't fix a code bug still causing crashes.

     StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath point at the user-writable data dir,
     NOT the root-owned code tree -- learned from the pending_uploads.jsonl
     PermissionError incident (2026-08-27) earlier in this same project. -->
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.eyeguard.findmycheck</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Applications/EyeGuard.app/Contents/Resources/python/bin/python3.12</string>
    <string>-m</string>
    <string>eyeguard.findmy_watcher</string>
    <string>--config</string>
    <string>/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard/config.yaml</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PYTHONPATH</key><string>/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard</string>
  </dict>
  <key>LimitLoadToSessionType</key><string>Aqua</string>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key><true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/Users/jonahdirrim/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard-data/findmy_watcher.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/jonahdirrim/Library/Application Support/EyeGuard-data/findmy_watcher.err.log</string>
</dict></plist>
